package hal

import "core/cpu"
import "core/ptr"

// Portable hardware abstraction layer. Nothing here requires an OS, allocator,
// or particular chip, and every package that imports it also compiles for
// hosted targets, so drivers can be tested on a desktop with
// `std/embedded/hal/fake`.
//
// Three layers, from the bottom up:
//
// 1. Registers. `Reg<T>` gives typed volatile access to one memory-mapped
//    register, and mask constants name its fields. `RegisterBlock` is a
//    peripheral's run of 32-bit registers at offsets from one base address.
//    Creating either is the single unsafe step; reading and writing an owned
//    handle afterwards is safe.
// 2. Interrupts. `Critical` masks interrupts on the current core until it is
//    dropped. It never excludes other threads or cores.
// 3. Device protocols. The interfaces below name what drivers need from a
//    device: OutputPin, InputPin, Delay, Counter, Alarm, I2c, Spi,
//    PwmOutput, PwmInput, AnalogInput, Can, I2sOutput, I2sInput, Watchdog,
//    and Registers; the polling forms I2cPoll and SpiPoll; the target
//    (slave) sides I2cTarget and SpiTarget; and DmaChannel, DmaBuffer, and
//    SpiDma for transfers that run while the program does other work. Any
//    value with matching methods implements them. Drivers are usually
//    generic over a bounded parameter, `fn probe<B: hal.I2c>(bus: &mut B)`, which is
//    statically dispatched and reports a wrong argument where it is passed.
//    `&mut dyn hal.I2c` instead dispatches at run time, for drivers chosen at
//    run time or a list of different devices. Serial ports implement the
//    `std/io` Reader and Writer interfaces, so ports work with `std/io`
//    helpers and `std/fmt` formatting unchanged, and the Port and PollPort
//    protocols of `std/embedded/hal/serial`, which stay out of this package
//    so that it depends on no stream package.
//
// Blocking and polling. The plain protocols wait until an operation is
// done. Their polling forms never wait: each call does what the hardware
// allows now and reports whether the operation has finished, so a program
// keeps working meanwhile, or an std/embedded/executor task returns Pending
// and is polled again on a timer or when the peripheral's interrupt raises
// a signal. A polled operation keeps its state in the peripheral value, not
// in borrowed buffers: every call passes the same arguments again until the
// operation finishes, fails, or is cancelled, and the buffers stay the
// caller's in between. Methods that start an operation are named poll_*,
// return `bool!Error` (true once finished) or an io.Poll for byte streams,
// and the operation is over after any error. `BlockingI2c` and
// `BlockingSpi` turn a polling implementation into a blocking one, so a
// controller that implements only the polling form serves both kinds of
// driver.
//
// Addresses are 7-bit I2C addresses; controllers that also reach 10-bit
// addresses offer that through methods of their own. Times are
// microseconds: counters and alarms count them in a u64, which no program
// outlives. Pin, delay, and counter operations cannot fail; alarm, bus, PWM,
// and analog operations report the shared
// `Error` below, because such a device may itself sit on a bus. A bus is
// configured when it is created, with a frequency and for SPI a `SpiMode`,
// and so is a PWM output, with its frequency; the protocols then only move
// bytes and duty cycles, so drivers never depend on how a device was set up.
//
// The register block protocol is for peripheral drivers rather than device
// drivers. Microcontroller vendors license the same peripheral designs, so
// one I2C, SPI, or UART controller appears in many chips at different
// addresses. `std/embedded/peripheral` drives such controllers through any
// register block: a `RegisterBlock` on the chip, or a simulated controller in
// a desktop test. Reads take `&mut self` because reading a device register can
// change it, for example by popping a FIFO.
//
// Chips and boards. A chip package (`std/embedded/chip/NAME`) turns one
// microcontroller's registers into values with the protocols above. A board
// package (`std/embedded/board/NAME`) configures its chip for one circuit
// board and names what is wired on it. Firmware builds select both with
// `board = "NAME"` in dodo.toml, and `import "std/embedded/board"` (or
// `std/embedded/chip` for a custom board) resolves to the selected package.
// Every board package provides the same entry points, so applications move
// between boards unchanged:
//
//    NAME: &str                    the board's name
//    take() -> Board!Error         start the chip once; Busy on a second call
//    Board.led                     the user LED, an output pin, on when high
//    Board.pins                    output(n) / input(n) -> Pin!Error per GPIO,
//                                  and i2c(sda, scl, hz),
//                                  spi(clock, tx, rx, mode, hz), and
//                                  uart(tx, rx, baud), with rx an
//                                  Option<u32>, for buses with the I2C and
//                                  SPI bus and serial port protocols on
//                                  those pins (Busy if a pin or the
//                                  controller is taken, InvalidInput if the
//                                  pins cannot carry the bus);
//                                  pwm(pin, hz) for a PWM output and
//                                  analog(pin) for an analog input on
//                                  that pin, and temperature() for the
//                                  chip's temperature sensor, an analog
//                                  input too;
//                                  release(pin), release_i2c(bus),
//                                  release_spi(bus), release_uart(port),
//                                  release_pwm(output), and
//                                  release_analog(input) give them back.
//                                  Chips may offer more,
//                                  as the RP2040 and RP2350 do: targets
//                                  (slaves) with i2c_target and spi_target
//                                  (I2cTarget, SpiTarget), pwm_input(pin,
//                                  window_us) for a PwmInput,
//                                  spi_with_select, and uart_flow. Buses
//                                  implement the polling forms too, and
//                                  serial ports serial.Port and
//                                  serial.PollPort
//    Board.timer                   the Delay and Counter protocols:
//                                  delay_us, delay_ms, now_us(&self) -> u64;
//                                  chips may hand out alarms with the Alarm
//                                  protocol that are also
//                                  std/embedded/executor clocks, as the
//                                  RP2040 and RP2350 do with alarm(n)
//    Board.watchdog                start(timeout_ms) -> void!Error, feed(),
//                                  stop(), with the Watchdog protocol:
//                                  resets the chip unless fed in time
//                                  (InvalidInput for a timeout of 0 or
//                                  above max_timeout_ms()); reset_reason()
//                                  tells whether it did, and the
//                                  scratch(n) words survive its reset
//    Board.usb                     serial() -> port!Error, a USB serial
//                                  port on the board's USB connector with
//                                  the serial port protocols (Busy if one
//                                  is open), and release_serial(port)
//    Board.dma                     chips with DMA hand out channels with
//                                  the DmaChannel protocol, as the RP2040
//                                  and RP2350 do with channel() and
//                                  release(channel)
//    Board.core1, Board.fifos,     on boards with a dual-core chip, as the
//    Board.spinlocks               RP2040 and RP2350 boards: start(stack,
//                                  task) runs a task on the second core,
//                                  and claim() hands out the cores' FIFO
//                                  ends and spinlocks
//    I2C_SDA_PIN, I2C_SCL_PIN      the board's usual I2C pins
//    SPI_CLOCK_PIN, SPI_TX_PIN,    the board's usual SPI pins, and a free
//    SPI_RX_PIN, SPI_SELECT_PIN    pin for one device's chip select
//    UART_TX_PIN, UART_RX_PIN      the board's usual serial port pins
//    reboot_to_bootloader()        restart into the board's flashing mode
//    report_panic(check, file,     unsafe; write a panic hook's failure to
//                 line, column)    the serial ports and the USB serial
//                                  port, which the default hook also does
//                                  before it halts

// Bus and device failures shared by every driver and chip implementation.
// A single payload-free enum keeps drivers composable and comparable with ==.
pub enum Error {
    // The device did not respond or a flag did not change in time.
    Timeout,
    // No device acknowledged its address or a data byte.
    NoAcknowledge,
    // Electrical or protocol fault, including lost bus arbitration.
    Bus,
    // Data arrived faster than it was consumed.
    Overrun,
    // The peripheral is in use or not ready for this request.
    Busy,
    // An argument is outside what the device or bus accepts.
    InvalidInput,
    // The implementation does not support this operation.
    Unsupported
}

pub fn error_name(error: Error) -> &str from(static) {
    match error {
        Error.Timeout => {
            return "Timeout"
        }
        Error.NoAcknowledge => {
            return "NoAcknowledge"
        }
        Error.Bus => {
            return "Bus"
        }
        Error.Overrun => {
            return "Overrun"
        }
        Error.Busy => {
            return "Busy"
        }
        Error.InvalidInput => {
            return "InvalidInput"
        }
        Error.Unsupported => {
            return "Unsupported"
        }
    }
}

// A digital output, such as a pin driving an LED or a chip select.
pub interface OutputPin {
    fn set_high(&mut self)
    fn set_low(&mut self)
}

// A digital input.
pub interface InputPin {
    fn is_high(&self) -> bool
}

// A busy-waiting delay source, such as a timer.
pub interface Delay {
    fn delay_us(&mut self, microseconds: u32)
}

// A free-running counter that keeps time, such as a chip's microsecond
// timer. Drivers measure intervals and give up on stuck devices with it,
// through Deadline and Ticker below, without naming a chip.
pub interface Counter {
    // Microseconds since some fixed start, such as the chip's start; never
    // decreases.
    fn now_us(&self) -> u64
}

// A one-shot alarm on a counter: it fires once when now_us() reaches the
// time it was given. Code polls `fired`, or a chip's interrupt for the
// alarm runs a handler, which calls `clear`. Raising that interrupt is
// chip-specific, as for the RP2040's and RP2350's `listen` and `irq`; the
// protocol only sets times and reports them. An alarm is also a Counter.
pub interface Alarm {
    // Microseconds on the counter the alarm compares with.
    fn now_us(&self) -> u64
    // Fire once when now_us() reaches `time_us`, replacing any earlier time
    // and clearing an earlier firing. A time already reached fires at once.
    // InvalidInput if the time lies further ahead than the alarm reaches,
    // which each implementation states.
    fn schedule_at_us(&mut self, time_us: u64) -> void!Error
    // Disarm: a scheduled time no longer fires. An earlier firing stays
    // reported until `clear`.
    fn cancel(&mut self)
    // True once the scheduled time was reached, until `clear` or the next
    // schedule.
    fn fired(&self) -> bool
    // Acknowledge a firing. An interrupt handler has to, or the interrupt
    // stays raised.
    fn clear(&mut self)
}

// An I2C bus controller. Addresses are 7-bit device addresses.
pub interface I2c {
    fn write(&mut self, address: u8, bytes: &[u8]) -> void!Error
    fn read(&mut self, address: u8, buffer: &mut[u8]) -> void!Error
    // Write `bytes`, then read `buffer.len` bytes without releasing the bus.
    fn write_read(&mut self, address: u8, bytes: &[u8], buffer: &mut[u8]) -> void!Error
}

// An SPI bus controller. Chip select is a separate output pin; see SpiDevice.
pub interface Spi {
    // Full-duplex exchange in place: each byte sent is replaced by the byte read.
    fn transfer(&mut self, bytes: &mut[u8]) -> void!Error
    fn write(&mut self, bytes: &[u8]) -> void!Error
}

// A pulse-width modulated output: a square wave that is high for
// duty / max_duty() of each period, which dims an LED, sets a motor's speed,
// or positions a servo. Its frequency is set when the output is created.
// A duty of 0 holds it low and max_duty() high.
pub interface PwmOutput {
    // The duty that holds the output high, at least 1.
    fn max_duty(&self) -> u16
    // InvalidInput if `duty` is above max_duty().
    fn set_duty(&mut self, duty: u16) -> void!Error
}

// An input of an analog-to-digital converter. Each read converts the voltage
// once: 0 at ground, max_value() at the converter's reference voltage, and
// proportionally in between.
pub interface AnalogInput {
    // The value read at the reference voltage, such as 4095 for a 12-bit
    // converter.
    fn max_value(&self) -> u16
    fn read(&mut self) -> u16!Error
}

// --- Polling forms -------------------------------------------------------
//
// See "Blocking and polling" in the package comment for the contract they
// share.

// An I2C bus controller whose transactions run while the program does
// other work. A transaction may be any length: an implementation moves as
// many bytes as its FIFO holds per poll and holds the bus, with SCL low,
// until the next poll continues it, which every I2C device accepts as clock
// stretching. Addresses are 7-bit device addresses, as for I2c.
pub interface I2cPoll {
    // Advance a write of `bytes` to the device at `address`, without
    // waiting: false while it runs, true once it has ended with a STOP. The
    // first call starts the transaction; later calls pass the same address
    // and bytes until it finishes. Errors as for I2c.write, after which the
    // transaction is over. InvalidInput for a call whose address or lengths
    // differ from the running transaction's, which keeps running.
    fn poll_write(&mut self, address: u8, bytes: &[u8]) -> bool!Error
    // Advance a read of `buffer.len` bytes, as for poll_write. The bytes
    // are all in `buffer` once it returns true; before that its contents
    // are unspecified.
    fn poll_read(&mut self, address: u8, buffer: &mut[u8]) -> bool!Error
    // Advance a write of `bytes`, a repeated START, and a read into
    // `buffer`, as one transaction, as I2c.write_read does.
    fn poll_write_read(&mut self, address: u8, bytes: &[u8], buffer: &mut[u8]) -> bool!Error
    // Abandon the running transaction, if any, ending it on the bus as
    // soon as the controller can, so the next poll starts a new one.
    fn cancel(&mut self)
}

// An SPI bus controller whose transfers run while the program does other
// work. Chip select stays a separate pin; SpiDevice drives it around
// polled transfers too.
pub interface SpiPoll {
    // Advance a full-duplex exchange of `bytes` in place, without waiting:
    // false while frames remain, true once every byte sent has been replaced
    // by the byte read. Later calls pass the same slice until it finishes;
    // InvalidInput for one of another length. The slice's bytes past the
    // ones exchanged so far are still to be sent, so the caller must not
    // change them meanwhile.
    fn poll_transfer(&mut self, bytes: &mut[u8]) -> bool!Error
    // Advance a write of `bytes`, discarding what arrives, as for
    // poll_transfer.
    fn poll_write(&mut self, bytes: &[u8]) -> bool!Error
    // Abandon the running transfer, if any, once the frame on the wire is
    // complete, and discard what was received.
    fn cancel(&mut self)
}

// --- Target (slave) sides ------------------------------------------------

// What happened on an I2C bus, from I2cTarget.poll.
pub enum I2cTargetEvent {
    // Nothing since the last poll.
    Idle,
    // The master wrote `count` bytes, now at the start of the buffer given
    // to `poll`. `start` is true when the first of them is the first byte
    // after the target's address, so it begins a new write, for example a
    // register number; the bytes of one poll never span two writes.
    Write(count: usize, start: bool),
    // The master reads and waits, with SCL held low, until `respond`
    // queues the bytes for it.
    Read,
    // A transaction addressed to the target ended with a STOP condition.
    Stop
}

// An I2C target (slave): a device on a bus that another master addresses.
// The address is set when the target is created. The program polls for
// what the master did, in order: written bytes, a STOP, and reads, which
// wait, with SCL held low, until `respond` answers them, so a slow program
// delays the master but loses nothing.
//
//     match target.poll(&mut buffer)? {
//         hal.I2cTargetEvent.Write(count, start) => { ... }
//         hal.I2cTargetEvent.Read => { target.respond(&reply)? }
//         hal.I2cTargetEvent.Stop => {}
//         hal.I2cTargetEvent.Idle => {}
//     }
pub interface I2cTarget {
    // What the master did since the last poll, without waiting: first the
    // bytes it wrote, as many as fit `buffer`, then a STOP, then a read
    // waiting for `respond`. Bus for an electrical or protocol fault, and
    // Overrun if written bytes were lost.
    fn poll(&mut self, buffer: &mut[u8]) -> I2cTargetEvent!Error
    // Answer a Read with `bytes`, as many as the controller holds; returns
    // how many. The master reads them one by one and may stop early; if it
    // reads on, the next poll reports another Read. An empty `bytes`
    // answers one 0xFF, what the master would read from an idle bus, so it
    // is never left waiting.
    fn respond(&mut self, bytes: &[u8]) -> usize!Error
}

// An SPI target (slave): another master drives the clock and the chip
// select, and the target shifts out what it queued while it shifts in what
// the master sends. Mode and frame size are set when it is created.
pub interface SpiTarget {
    // Queue `bytes` to send as the master clocks them, as many as there is
    // room for, without waiting; returns how many.
    fn queue(&mut self, bytes: &[u8]) -> usize!Error
    // The bytes the master sent since the last call, up to `buffer.len`,
    // without waiting; returns how many. Overrun if some were lost.
    fn read(&mut self, buffer: &mut[u8]) -> usize!Error
}

// --- PWM input capture ---------------------------------------------------

// One measurement of a pulse train from a PwmInput: how long a period
// lasts and how long the signal is high in it, in nanoseconds, averaged
// over `cycles` whole periods. Hardware that timestamps edges measures
// single periods (cycles 1); hardware that counts edges over a window
// averages many. A measurement that saw no whole period, such as a line
// that did not change, has cycles 0: period_ns is then how long the line
// was watched and high_ns how long of that it was high, so `duty` still
// reads the share of time it was high (100% or 0% for a steady line).
pub struct PulseCapture {
    pub period_ns: u64
    pub high_ns: u64
    pub cycles: u32

    // An empty measurement, for poll_capture to fill.
    pub fn new() -> PulseCapture {
        return PulseCapture { period_ns: 0, high_ns: 0, cycles: 0 }
    }

    // The signal's frequency in millihertz, rounded; 0 for a steady line.
    pub fn frequency_millihz(&self) -> u64 {
        if self.cycles == 0 || self.period_ns == 0 {
            return 0
        }
        return (1_000_000_000_000u64 + self.period_ns / 2) / self.period_ns
    }

    // The signal's frequency in hertz, rounded; 0 for a steady line.
    pub fn frequency_hz(&self) -> u32 {
        millihertz := self.frequency_millihz()
        hertz := (millihertz + 500) / 1000
        if hertz > 0xFFFF_FFFFu64 {
            return 0xFFFF_FFFF
        }
        return hertz as u32
    }

    // The high time as a share of the period, scaled to 0..`max` and
    // rounded: `duty(100)` is a percentage, and a PwmOutput's max_duty()
    // reproduces the signal on an output.
    pub fn duty(&self, max: u32) -> u32 {
        if self.period_ns == 0 {
            return 0
        }
        high := self.high_ns
        if high > self.period_ns {
            high = self.period_ns
        }
        // 128-bit headroom is not needed: periods beyond 2^32 ns (4.3 s)
        // lose low bits first.
        period := self.period_ns
        scale := max as u64
        for high > 0xFFFF_FFFFu64 || period > 0xFFFF_FFFFu64 {
            high = high >> 1
            period = period >> 1
        }
        return ((high * scale + period / 2) / period) as u32
    }

    // The high time in microseconds, rounded, as a servo or RC receiver's
    // pulse length is usually given.
    // Saturates at the largest u32 (about 71 minutes).
    pub fn high_us(&self) -> u32 {
        return saturate_us(self.high_ns)
    }

    // The period in microseconds, rounded, saturating as high_us does.
    pub fn period_us(&self) -> u32 {
        return saturate_us(self.period_ns)
    }
}

fn saturate_us(nanoseconds: u64) -> u32 {
    microseconds := nanoseconds / 1000 + (nanoseconds % 1000 + 500) / 1000
    if microseconds > 0xFFFF_FFFFu64 {
        return 0xFFFF_FFFF
    }
    return microseconds as u32
}

// An input that measures the pulses on a pin: the frequency and duty cycle
// of a PWM signal, a fan's tachometer, or the pulse length of a servo
// signal. Like a PWM output it is configured when it is created, with how
// long one measurement may take. Measurements follow one another
// continuously.
pub interface PwmInput {
    // Without waiting: true with `capture` set to a measurement that
    // finished since the last call, false while none has. A program that
    // polls more slowly than measurements finish gets the latest. Overrun
    // if a measurement was lost, as when a counter overflowed; the next
    // measurement has then already begun.
    fn poll_capture(&mut self, capture: &mut PulseCapture) -> bool!Error
}

// --- CAN -----------------------------------------------------------------

// The largest identifiers: 11-bit standard and 29-bit extended.
pub const CAN_MAX_STANDARD_ID: u32 = 0x7FF
pub const CAN_MAX_EXTENDED_ID: u32 = 0x1FFF_FFFF
// The most data bytes of a classic CAN frame and of a CAN FD frame.
pub const CAN_MAX_DATA: usize = 8
pub const CAN_FD_MAX_DATA: usize = 64

// The data length of a CAN frame with data length code `dlc`, 0 to 15:
// codes up to 8 are lengths, and on CAN FD 9 to 15 mean 12, 16, 20, 24,
// 32, 48, and 64 bytes. A classic frame with a code above 8 carries 8.
pub fn can_dlc_length(dlc: u8, fd: bool) -> usize {
    code := dlc & 0xF
    if code <= 8 {
        return code as usize
    }
    if !fd {
        return 8
    }
    if code <= 12 {
        return 8 + (code as usize - 8) * 4
    }
    if code == 13 {
        return 32
    }
    if code == 14 {
        return 48
    }
    return 64
}

// The data length code for `length` bytes: InvalidInput above 64, or for
// a length CAN FD cannot carry exactly (it pads to 12, 16, 20, 24, 32, 48,
// or 64 bytes).
pub fn can_length_dlc(length: usize) -> u8!Error {
    if length <= 8 {
        return ok(length as u8)
    }
    for code in 9u8..16 {
        if can_dlc_length(code, true) == length {
            return ok(code)
        }
    }
    return err(Error.InvalidInput)
}

// One CAN frame: an identifier, standard or extended, and up to 8 data
// bytes, or 64 on CAN FD, or a remote request for that many. The fields
// are checked as they are set, so every frame is one a controller can
// send. Frames are values a program keeps and reuses:
//
//     frame := hal.CanFrame.new()
//     frame.set_data(0x123, false, &payload)?
//     can.poll_send(&frame)?
//     if can.poll_receive(&mut frame)? { ... frame.id(), frame.bytes() ... }
pub struct CanFrame {
    id: u32
    extended: bool
    remote: bool
    fd: bool
    bit_rate_switch: bool
    length: usize
    data: [64]u8

    // A data frame with standard identifier 0 and no data, for a receive
    // to fill or a set_* call to change.
    pub fn new() -> CanFrame {
        return CanFrame { id: 0, extended: false, remote: false, fd: false, bit_rate_switch: false, length: 0, data: [0; 64] }
    }

    // A classic data frame with standard identifier `id` and `bytes` as
    // its data; see set_data.
    pub fn standard(id: u32, bytes: &[u8]) -> CanFrame!Error {
        frame := CanFrame.new()
        frame.set_data(id, false, bytes)?
        return ok(frame)
    }

    // A classic data frame with extended identifier `id`; see set_data.
    pub fn extended(id: u32, bytes: &[u8]) -> CanFrame!Error {
        frame := CanFrame.new()
        frame.set_data(id, true, bytes)?
        return ok(frame)
    }

    // Make this a classic data frame: identifier `id`, extended or
    // standard, with `bytes` as its data. InvalidInput for an identifier
    // too large for its kind or more than CAN_MAX_DATA bytes; the frame is
    // then unchanged.
    pub fn set_data(&mut self, id: u32, extended: bool, bytes: &[u8]) -> void!Error {
        check_can_id(id, extended)?
        if bytes.len > CAN_MAX_DATA {
            return err(Error.InvalidInput)
        }
        self.fill(id, extended, false, false, false, bytes)
        return ok()
    }

    // Make this a remote frame, a request for `length` bytes of data
    // frame `id`, 0 to CAN_MAX_DATA. Errors as for set_data.
    pub fn set_remote(&mut self, id: u32, extended: bool, length: usize) -> void!Error {
        check_can_id(id, extended)?
        if length > CAN_MAX_DATA {
            return err(Error.InvalidInput)
        }
        none: [0]u8 = []
        self.fill(id, extended, true, false, false, &none)
        self.length = length
        return ok()
    }

    // Make this a CAN FD data frame with `bytes` as its data, sent with the
    // faster data bit rate if `bit_rate_switch`. InvalidInput for a length
    // can_length_dlc rejects, or as for set_data.
    pub fn set_fd(&mut self, id: u32, extended: bool, bytes: &[u8], bit_rate_switch: bool) -> void!Error {
        check_can_id(id, extended)?
        can_length_dlc(bytes.len)?
        self.fill(id, extended, false, true, bit_rate_switch, bytes)
        return ok()
    }

    fn fill(&mut self, id: u32, extended: bool, remote: bool, fd: bool, bit_rate_switch: bool, bytes: &[u8]) {
        self.id = id
        self.extended = extended
        self.remote = remote
        self.fd = fd
        self.bit_rate_switch = bit_rate_switch
        self.length = bytes.len
        for index in 0..bytes.len {
            self.data[index] = bytes[index]
        }
    }

    pub fn id(&self) -> u32 {
        return self.id
    }

    pub fn is_extended(&self) -> bool {
        return self.extended
    }

    pub fn is_remote(&self) -> bool {
        return self.remote
    }

    pub fn is_fd(&self) -> bool {
        return self.fd
    }

    pub fn bit_rate_switch(&self) -> bool {
        return self.bit_rate_switch
    }

    // The data length: of the data, or for a remote frame the length it
    // requests.
    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
        return self.length
    }

    // The frame's data length code.
    pub fn dlc(&self) -> u8 {
        match can_length_dlc(self.length) {
            ok(code) => {
                return code
            }
            err(_) => {
                return 15
            }
        }
    }

    // The data bytes; empty for a remote frame.
    pub fn bytes(&self) -> &[u8] {
        if self.remote {
            return self.data[0..0]
        }
        return self.data[0..self.length]
    }

    // Whether this frame equals `other`: identifier and its kind, remote,
    // FD, bit rate switch, length, and data.
    pub fn same(&self, other: &CanFrame) -> bool {
        if self.id != other.id || self.extended != other.extended || self.remote != other.remote || self.fd != other.fd || self.bit_rate_switch != other.bit_rate_switch || self.length != other.length {
            return false
        }
        if self.remote {
            return true
        }
        for index in 0..self.length {
            if self.data[index] != other.data[index] {
                return false
            }
        }
        return true
    }

    // Make this frame a copy of `other`.
    pub fn copy_from(&mut self, other: &CanFrame) {
        self.id = other.id
        self.extended = other.extended
        self.remote = other.remote
        self.fd = other.fd
        self.bit_rate_switch = other.bit_rate_switch
        self.length = other.length
        for index in 0..other.length {
            self.data[index] = other.data[index]
        }
    }
}

fn check_can_id(id: u32, extended: bool) -> void!Error {
    if extended {
        if id > CAN_MAX_EXTENDED_ID {
            return err(Error.InvalidInput)
        }
    } else if id > CAN_MAX_STANDARD_ID {
        return err(Error.InvalidInput)
    }
    return ok()
}

// An acceptance filter: frames whose identifier bits under `mask` equal
// those of `id`, of the same kind (standard or extended), pass. A mask of 0
// passes every frame of that kind. Controllers configure their hardware
// filters from these when they are created.
pub struct CanFilter {
    pub id: u32
    pub mask: u32
    pub extended: bool

    // A filter that passes exactly identifier `id`.
    pub fn exact(id: u32, extended: bool) -> CanFilter {
        mask := CAN_MAX_STANDARD_ID
        if extended {
            mask = CAN_MAX_EXTENDED_ID
        }
        return CanFilter { id, mask, extended }
    }

    // A filter that passes every frame of one kind.
    pub fn all(extended: bool) -> CanFilter {
        return CanFilter { id: 0, mask: 0, extended }
    }

    pub fn accepts(&self, frame: &CanFrame) -> bool {
        return frame.is_extended() == self.extended && (frame.id() & self.mask) == (self.id & self.mask)
    }
}

// Where a CAN controller stands in the bus's fault confinement, from its
// transmit and receive error counters. An error-active node signals the
// errors it sees; an error-passive one, after many errors, only listens for
// them; a bus-off node, after very many, takes no part until it recovers
// by seeing 128 runs of 11 recessive bits.
pub enum CanErrorState {
    Active,
    Passive,
    BusOff
}

// A CAN controller on a bus. Bit rate, filters, and modes are set when it
// is created. Frames queue in the controller's buffers in both directions,
// so both methods return at once.
pub interface Can {
    // Queue `frame` to send, without waiting: true once queued, false while
    // every transmit buffer is full. Unsupported for an FD frame on a
    // classic controller, Bus while the controller is bus-off.
    fn poll_send(&mut self, frame: &CanFrame) -> bool!Error
    // Without waiting: true with the oldest received frame in `frame`,
    // false while none waits. Overrun once if frames were lost since the
    // last call, before the frames that followed them.
    fn poll_receive(&mut self, frame: &mut CanFrame) -> bool!Error
    // Where the controller stands in fault confinement. Errors if the
    // controller cannot be asked, as one on a failing SPI bus.
    fn error_state(&mut self) -> CanErrorState!Error
}

// One bit time of a CAN controller in time quanta of `prescaler` input
// clock cycles: a sync segment of one quantum, `segment1` (propagation
// plus phase segment 1) before the sample point, and `segment2` after it.
// `sync_jump` is how far the controller may move the sample point to
// follow the transmitter. Controllers program their timing registers from
// it, each with its own encoding.
pub struct CanBitTiming {
    pub prescaler: u32
    pub segment1: u32
    pub segment2: u32
    pub sync_jump: u32

    // Time quanta per bit.
    pub fn quanta(&self) -> u32 {
        return 1 + self.segment1 + self.segment2
    }

    // The resulting bit rate from an input clock of `clock_hz`, rounded.
    pub fn bitrate(&self, clock_hz: u32) -> u32 {
        cycles := self.prescaler as u64 * self.quanta() as u64
        return ((clock_hz as u64 + cycles / 2) / cycles) as u32
    }

    // Where the sample point falls, in thousandths of the bit.
    pub fn sample_point_permille(&self) -> u32 {
        return ((1 + self.segment1) * 1000 + self.quanta() / 2) / self.quanta()
    }
}

// The ranges a controller's timing registers accept, for can_timing.
pub struct CanTimingLimits {
    pub prescaler_min: u32
    pub prescaler_max: u32
    // Prescalers must be a multiple of this, such as 2 for controllers that
    // count the clock in pairs.
    pub prescaler_step: u32
    pub segment1_min: u32
    pub segment1_max: u32
    pub segment2_min: u32
    pub segment2_max: u32
    pub sync_jump_max: u32
}

// The bit timing for `bitrate` from an input clock of `clock_hz`, within
// `limits`, with the sample point closest to `sample_point_permille`
// (875, 87.5%, is CiA's recommendation up to 800 kbit/s, 750 at 1 Mbit/s).
// The exact bit rate is preferred; among timings as exact, the sample point
// closest to the request, then the most quanta, which follow the bus most
// finely. The synchronization jump
// width is as wide as segment2 and the limits allow. InvalidInput if no
// timing reaches the bit rate within 0.5%.
pub fn can_timing(clock_hz: u32, bitrate: u32, sample_point_permille: u32, limits: &CanTimingLimits) -> CanBitTiming!Error {
    if bitrate == 0 || clock_hz == 0 || sample_point_permille == 0 || sample_point_permille >= 1000 {
        return err(Error.InvalidInput)
    }
    step := limits.prescaler_step
    if step == 0 {
        step = 1
    }
    smallest_quanta := 1 + limits.segment1_min + limits.segment2_min
    largest_quanta := 1 + limits.segment1_max + limits.segment2_max
    found := false
    best_error := 0xFFFF_FFFF_FFFF_FFFFu64
    best_sample := 0xFFFF_FFFFu32
    best_quanta := 0u32
    best := CanBitTiming { prescaler: 0, segment1: 0, segment2: 0, sync_jump: 0 }
    prescaler := limits.prescaler_min
    if prescaler == 0 {
        prescaler = step
    }
    for prescaler <= limits.prescaler_max {
        if prescaler % step == 0 {
            quanta := (clock_hz as u64 + (prescaler as u64 * bitrate as u64) / 2) / (prescaler as u64 * bitrate as u64)
            if quanta >= smallest_quanta as u64 && quanta <= largest_quanta as u64 {
                actual := clock_hz as u64 / (prescaler as u64 * quanta)
                // The bit-rate error in millionths.
                difference := 0u64
                if actual > bitrate as u64 {
                    difference = actual - bitrate as u64
                } else {
                    difference = bitrate as u64 - actual
                }
                exact := clock_hz as u64 % (prescaler as u64 * quanta) == 0
                error := difference * 1_000_000 / bitrate as u64
                if !exact && error == 0 {
                    error = 1
                }
                if error <= 5000 {
                    total := quanta as u32
                    // Segment 2 from the sample point, within the limits.
                    after := (total * (1000 - sample_point_permille) + 500) / 1000
                    if after < limits.segment2_min {
                        after = limits.segment2_min
                    }
                    if after > limits.segment2_max {
                        after = limits.segment2_max
                    }
                    before := total - 1 - after
                    if before > limits.segment1_max {
                        before = limits.segment1_max
                        after = total - 1 - before
                    }
                    if before >= limits.segment1_min && after >= limits.segment2_min && after <= limits.segment2_max {
                        point := ((1 + before) * 1000 + total / 2) / total
                        miss := 0u32
                        if point > sample_point_permille {
                            miss = point - sample_point_permille
                        } else {
                            miss = sample_point_permille - point
                        }
                        better := !found || error < best_error || (error == best_error && (miss < best_sample || (miss == best_sample && total > best_quanta)))
                        if better {
                            jump := after
                            if jump > limits.sync_jump_max {
                                jump = limits.sync_jump_max
                            }
                            if jump == 0 {
                                jump = 1
                            }
                            best = CanBitTiming { prescaler, segment1: before, segment2: after, sync_jump: jump }
                            best_error = error
                            best_sample = miss
                            best_quanta = total
                            found = true
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if prescaler == limits.prescaler_max {
            break
        }
        prescaler += 1
    }
    if !found {
        return err(Error.InvalidInput)
    }
    return ok(best)
}

// --- I2S -----------------------------------------------------------------

// How an I2S stream frames its samples. The word select (LRCLK) line marks
// which channel a sample belongs to: low for the left channel in Philips
// framing, high for it in both justified framings.
pub enum I2sFormat {
    // The Philips I2S standard: word select low for the left channel, and
    // each sample starts, most significant bit first, one bit clock after
    // word select changes.
    Philips,
    // Word select high for the left channel, and each sample starts on the
    // bit clock where word select changes.
    LeftJustified,
    // Word select high for the left channel, and each sample ends on the
    // bit clock before word select changes, sign-extended above in a wider
    // slot.
    RightJustified
}

// Samples are i32 values with the audio sample in the most significant
// bits: a 16-bit sample is `value << 16`, so full scale is the same at
// every resolution and an implementation keeps the top bits it sends.
// Stereo samples interleave, left first. Rate, resolution, channels, and
// format are set when a stream is created.

// An I2S output: samples that a DAC or amplifier plays at the stream's
// sample rate. The implementation keeps a queue and plays silence when it
// runs dry, so a program keeps the queue topped up from its poll.
pub interface I2sOutput {
    // Frames per second.
    fn sample_rate(&self) -> u32
    // Samples per frame: 1 or 2.
    fn channels(&self) -> u32
    // Queue samples to play, without waiting: as many whole frames as there
    // is room for; returns how many samples. InvalidInput for a slice that
    // is not whole frames.
    fn poll_write(&mut self, samples: &[i32]) -> usize!Error
}

// An I2S input: samples a microphone or ADC sends at the stream's sample
// rate.
pub interface I2sInput {
    fn sample_rate(&self) -> u32
    fn channels(&self) -> u32
    // The samples received since the last call, as many whole frames as
    // fit `samples`, without waiting; returns how many samples. Overrun
    // once if samples were lost because they were not read in time.
    fn poll_read(&mut self, samples: &mut[i32]) -> usize!Error
}

// A watchdog: once started, it resets the chip unless the program feeds it
// again within its timeout, so firmware that hangs restarts instead of
// staying stuck. Its timeout is set when it is started; drivers and tasks
// that run long loops only feed it.
pub interface Watchdog {
    // Count down from the timeout again.
    fn feed(&mut self)
}

// Why the chip last started, as its watchdog reports it.
pub enum ResetReason {
    // Not the watchdog: power-up, the reset pin, a debugger, or the boot
    // ROM, for example after flashing.
    Other,
    // The watchdog was not fed before its timeout, or a panic halted the
    // program while the watchdog ran.
    Timeout,
    // The program reset the chip through the watchdog on purpose.
    Restart
}

pub fn reset_reason_name(reason: ResetReason) -> &str from(static) {
    match reason {
        ResetReason.Other => {
            return "Other"
        }
        ResetReason.Timeout => {
            return "Timeout"
        }
        ResetReason.Restart => {
            return "Restart"
        }
    }
}

// A peripheral's 32-bit registers at byte offsets, for peripheral drivers.
pub interface Registers {
    fn read(&mut self, offset: usize) -> u32
    fn write(&mut self, offset: usize, value: u32)
}

// The size of each element a DMA transfer moves.
pub enum DmaWidth {
    Byte,
    HalfWord,
    Word
}

// What paces a DMA transfer.
pub enum DmaPace {
    // Nothing: the channel moves elements as fast as the bus allows, as
    // for a copy between two buffers.
    Unpaced,
    // The peripheral register the transfer writes to: an element moves
    // whenever the peripheral has room, such as a UART's transmit FIFO.
    Destination,
    // The peripheral register the transfer reads from: an element moves
    // whenever the peripheral has one, such as a UART's receive FIFO.
    Source
}

// An address that wraps around a ring of `1 << bits` bytes, aligned to its
// size, instead of running on: a receiver keeps writing into the same
// buffer, or a transmitter keeps reading from it.
pub enum DmaRing {
    None,
    Source(u32),
    Destination(u32)
}

// One DMA transfer: `count` elements of `width` from `source` to
// `destination`. An address that increments advances by one element after
// each; one that does not stays on a peripheral register.
pub struct DmaTransfer {
    pub source: usize
    pub destination: usize
    pub count: u32
    pub width: DmaWidth
    pub increment_source: bool
    pub increment_destination: bool
    pub pace: DmaPace
    pub ring: DmaRing
}

// The address of `bytes`' first byte, for a DMA transfer's source or
// destination. Taking it is safe; the channel's start is the unsafe step.
pub fn address_of(bytes: &[u8]) -> usize {
    // SAFETY: the pointer becomes a number and is never dereferenced here.
    unsafe {
        return ptr.as_ptr(bytes) as usize
    }
}

// A DMA channel: moves data between memory and peripherals while the
// processor does other work. Peripheral drivers start transfers through it
// and chip packages implement it, so drivers that use DMA name no chip.
// Drivers keep their buffers borrowed until the transfer is done or
// aborted, as starting one is unsafe; applications use their safe methods.
pub interface DmaChannel {
    // Start `transfer`. InvalidInput if the channel cannot pace by the
    // peripheral named, wrap a ring of that size, or move that many
    // elements; Busy if a transfer is still running.
    // SAFETY: the memory the transfer reads and writes must stay valid,
    // and nothing else may access the memory it writes, until it is done or
    // aborted.
    unsafe fn start(&mut self, transfer: DmaTransfer) -> void!Error
    // True while a transfer is running.
    fn is_busy(&mut self) -> bool
    // The elements the running transfer has yet to move: 0 once it is done.
    fn remaining(&mut self) -> u32
    // The largest count a transfer may have.
    fn max_count(&mut self) -> u32
    // Stop the running transfer, if any, and wait until the channel no
    // longer touches memory.
    fn abort(&mut self)
    // Bus if a transfer since the last call hit a bus error, such as an
    // address with nothing behind it, which stops it.
    fn take_error(&mut self) -> void!Error
}

// A transfer between a buffer and a peripheral that runs while the program
// does other work, usually by DMA, in one direction fixed when the session
// is created. The session holds the buffer and lends it out only between
// transfers, so the program never touches memory a transfer is moving:
//
//     transfer.buffer()?[0] = command    // fill it, then:
//     transfer.start(count)?
//     // ... other work, or return Pending from a task ...
//     if transfer.poll_finish()? { ... read transfer.buffer()? ... }
//
// DmaStream implements it over any DmaChannel; peripheral drivers build
// their DMA sessions on it, and fake.DmaBuffer stands in for one in tests.
pub interface DmaBuffer {
    // The buffer, to fill before a transfer or read after one. Busy while
    // a transfer runs.
    fn buffer(&mut self) -> &mut[u8]!Error
    // The buffer's length in bytes, the most a transfer moves.
    fn capacity(&self) -> usize
    // Start moving buffer[..count], and return at once. InvalidInput for
    // 0, more than the capacity, or a count the session cannot move (not a
    // whole number of its elements); Busy while a transfer runs.
    fn start(&mut self, count: usize) -> void!Error
    // Without waiting: true once no transfer runs, false while one does.
    // Bus if the transfer hit a bus error, Overrun if the peripheral lost
    // data; either way it is over.
    fn poll_finish(&mut self) -> bool!Error
    // Stop the running transfer, if any; the peripheral saw only part of
    // it.
    fn abort(&mut self)
}

// An SPI bus whose transfers run by DMA over a buffer the session holds,
// as DmaBuffer does, in both directions at once. A display driver streams
// a frame with start_write; a flash driver reads a page with
// start_transfer.
pub interface SpiDma {
    fn buffer(&mut self) -> &mut[u8]!Error
    fn capacity(&self) -> usize
    // Start sending buffer[..count] and replacing each byte with the one
    // received meanwhile. Errors as for DmaBuffer.start.
    fn start_transfer(&mut self, count: usize) -> void!Error
    // Start sending buffer[..count], discarding what arrives; the buffer
    // stays as it is.
    fn start_write(&mut self, count: usize) -> void!Error
    fn poll_finish(&mut self) -> bool!Error
    fn abort(&mut self)
}

// The number of bytes in one element of `width`.
pub fn dma_width_bytes(width: &DmaWidth) -> usize {
    match width {
        DmaWidth.Byte => {
            return 1
        }
        DmaWidth.HalfWord => {
            return 2
        }
        DmaWidth.Word => {
            return 4
        }
    }
}

fn copy_width(width: &DmaWidth) -> DmaWidth {
    match width {
        DmaWidth.Byte => {
            return DmaWidth.Byte
        }
        DmaWidth.HalfWord => {
            return DmaWidth.HalfWord
        }
        DmaWidth.Word => {
            return DmaWidth.Word
        }
    }
}

// A buffer moved to or from one peripheral data register by a DMA channel:
// the part every DMA-backed peripheral driver shares, written once against
// DmaChannel so it works with any chip's channels. It holds the channel and
// the buffer until it is dropped and implements DmaBuffer. Each transfer
// moves elements of the stream's width between the buffer, advancing, and
// the register, staying, paced by the peripheral's data request.
//
// The peripheral driver around it turns the peripheral's DMA request on
// before `start` and off after the transfer, and adds its own errors, as
// pl022.DmaBus does for SPI. Dropping a stream aborts a running transfer.
pub struct DmaStream: DmaBuffer {
    channel: &mut dyn DmaChannel
    buffer: &mut[u8]
    register: usize
    width: DmaWidth
    receive: bool
    running: bool

    // A stream that sends `buffer` to the peripheral register at
    // `register`, its address as the channel sees it, `width` at a time,
    // whenever the peripheral asks for data. InvalidInput for an empty
    // buffer or one not aligned to the width; Busy if the channel is
    // running.
    pub fn transmit(channel: &mut dyn DmaChannel, buffer: &mut[u8], register: usize, width: DmaWidth) -> DmaStream!Error from(channel, buffer) {
        return DmaStream.open(channel, buffer, register, width, false)
    }

    // A stream that fills `buffer` from the peripheral register at
    // `register` whenever the peripheral has data; errors as for transmit.
    pub fn receive(channel: &mut dyn DmaChannel, buffer: &mut[u8], register: usize, width: DmaWidth) -> DmaStream!Error from(channel, buffer) {
        return DmaStream.open(channel, buffer, register, width, true)
    }

    fn open(channel: &mut dyn DmaChannel, buffer: &mut[u8], register: usize, width: DmaWidth, receive: bool) -> DmaStream!Error from(channel, buffer) {
        size := dma_width_bytes(&width)
        if buffer.len == 0 || address_of(buffer) % size != 0 {
            return err(Error.InvalidInput)
        }
        if channel.is_busy() {
            return err(Error.Busy)
        }
        return ok(DmaStream { channel, buffer, register, width, receive, running: false })
    }

    // The buffer, between transfers; Busy while one runs.
    pub fn buffer(&mut self) -> &mut[u8]!Error from(self) {
        if self.running {
            return err(Error.Busy)
        }
        return ok(&mut self.buffer[..])
    }

    pub fn capacity(&self) -> usize {
        return self.buffer.len
    }

    // True for a stream that fills its buffer from the peripheral.
    pub fn is_receive(&self) -> bool {
        return self.receive
    }

    // The peripheral register the stream moves data to or from.
    pub fn register(&self) -> usize {
        return self.register
    }

    // Start moving buffer[..count]; see DmaBuffer.start. InvalidInput also
    // for a count that is not a whole number of elements or more elements
    // than the channel moves at once.
    pub fn start(&mut self, count: usize) -> void!Error {
        if self.running {
            return err(Error.Busy)
        }
        size := dma_width_bytes(&self.width)
        if count == 0 || count > self.buffer.len || count % size != 0 {
            return err(Error.InvalidInput)
        }
        elements := count / size
        if elements as u64 > self.channel.max_count() as u64 {
            return err(Error.InvalidInput)
        }
        start := address_of(self.buffer)
        transfer := DmaTransfer {
            source: start,
            destination: self.register,
            count: elements as u32,
            width: copy_width(&self.width),
            increment_source: true,
            increment_destination: false,
            pace: DmaPace.Destination,
            ring: DmaRing.None,
        }
        if self.receive {
            transfer = DmaTransfer {
                source: self.register,
                destination: start,
                count: elements as u32,
                width: copy_width(&self.width),
                increment_source: false,
                increment_destination: true,
                pace: DmaPace.Source,
                ring: DmaRing.None,
            }
        }
        // The fence makes the buffer's contents visible to the channel.
        cpu.fence()
        // SAFETY: the stream holds the buffer and lends it out again only
        // once the channel is done or aborted.
        unsafe {
            self.channel.start(transfer)?
        }
        self.running = true
        return ok()
    }

    // True while a transfer runs.
    pub fn is_busy(&mut self) -> bool {
        return self.running && self.channel.is_busy()
    }

    // The bytes the running transfer has yet to move: 0 once it is done.
    pub fn remaining(&mut self) -> usize {
        if !self.running {
            return 0
        }
        return self.channel.remaining() as usize * dma_width_bytes(&self.width)
    }

    // See DmaBuffer.poll_finish.
    pub fn poll_finish(&mut self) -> bool!Error {
        if !self.running {
            return ok(true)
        }
        if self.channel.is_busy() {
            return ok(false)
        }
        self.running = false
        // Makes what the channel wrote visible to the program.
        cpu.fence()
        self.channel.take_error()?
        return ok(true)
    }

    // Wait until the running transfer, if any, is done; errors as for
    // poll_finish.
    pub fn finish(&mut self) -> void!Error {
        for !self.poll_finish()? {}
        return ok()
    }

    // Stop the running transfer, if any.
    pub fn abort(&mut self) {
        if self.running {
            self.channel.abort()
            self.running = false
            cpu.fence()
            // An aborted transfer has nothing to report.
            match self.channel.take_error() {
                ok() => {}
                err(_) => {}
            }
        }
    }

    fn drop(&mut self) {
        self.abort()
    }
}

// Copy `source` into `destination` with a DMA channel and wait until it is
// done: unpaced, in words where both addresses and the length allow.
// InvalidInput if the lengths differ or are too many elements for the
// channel, Busy if it is running, Bus for a bus error.
pub fn dma_copy<C: DmaChannel>(channel: &mut C, destination: &mut[u8], source: &[u8]) -> void!Error {
    if destination.len != source.len {
        return err(Error.InvalidInput)
    }
    if source.len == 0 {
        return ok()
    }
    if channel.is_busy() {
        return err(Error.Busy)
    }
    origin := address_of(source)
    to := address_of(destination)
    width := DmaWidth.Byte
    size := 1usize
    if origin % 4 == 0 && to % 4 == 0 && source.len % 4 == 0 {
        width = DmaWidth.Word
        size = 4
    } else if origin % 2 == 0 && to % 2 == 0 && source.len % 2 == 0 {
        width = DmaWidth.HalfWord
        size = 2
    }
    elements := source.len / size
    if elements as u64 > channel.max_count() as u64 {
        return err(Error.InvalidInput)
    }
    transfer := DmaTransfer {
        source: origin,
        destination: to,
        count: elements as u32,
        width,
        increment_source: true,
        increment_destination: true,
        pace: DmaPace.Unpaced,
        ring: DmaRing.None,
    }
    cpu.fence()
    // SAFETY: both slices stay borrowed until the channel is done.
    unsafe {
        channel.start(transfer)?
    }
    for channel.is_busy() {}
    cpu.fence()
    return channel.take_error()
}

// Common I2C bus frequencies: standard mode, fast mode, and fast mode plus.
// Every device supports standard mode; most sensors also fast mode.
pub const I2C_STANDARD_HZ: u32 = 100_000
pub const I2C_FAST_HZ: u32 = 400_000
pub const I2C_FAST_PLUS_HZ: u32 = 1_000_000

// SPI clock polarity and phase, as numbered in device datasheets. Mode 0,
// clock idle low and data sampled on the rising edge, is the most common.
pub enum SpiMode {
    // Clock idles low; data is sampled on the rising (first) edge.
    Mode0,
    // Clock idles low; data is sampled on the falling (second) edge.
    Mode1,
    // Clock idles high; data is sampled on the falling (first) edge.
    Mode2,
    // Clock idles high; data is sampled on the rising (second) edge.
    Mode3
}

// Register fields are plain mask constants of the register's type, as in a
// datasheet or C header, so they cost nothing and can be reused freely:
//
//     const ENABLE: u32 = 1 << 0
//     const MODE: u32 = 0b111 << 4
//
// A mask must be nonzero and its bits contiguous.

// The lowest set bit of a nonzero mask, the factor that moves a field value
// into register position. Division and multiplication by it compile to shifts.
fn unit<T>(mask: T) -> T {
    one: T = 1
    return mask & ~(mask - one)
}

// `value` moved into the position of `mask`. Traps if it does not fit, so a
// wrong value never silently changes a neighbouring field.
pub fn encode<T>(mask: T, value: T) -> T {
    step := unit(mask)
    core.assert(value <= mask / step)
    return value * step
}

// The field `mask` extracted from a whole register value.
pub fn decode<T>(mask: T, register: T) -> T {
    return (register & mask) / unit(mask)
}

// One memory-mapped register holding T (u8, u16, u32, or u64 up to the target
// pointer width). Every access is a single volatile load or store of T; the
// compiler never merges, removes, or splits it.
//
// A Reg is move-only. Owning it is the permission to touch that register, so
// build peripheral structs from Reg fields and hand them out once.
pub struct Reg<T> {
    address: usize

    // SAFETY: `address` must be a valid, T-aligned device register for this
    // target, accessible with T-sized loads and stores, and no other handle
    // or code may access it in a conflicting way while this handle exists.
    pub unsafe fn at(address: usize) -> Reg<T> {
        return Reg<T> { address }
    }

    pub fn address(&self) -> usize {
        return self.address
    }

    pub fn read(&self) -> T {
        // SAFETY: `at` guarantees a valid register owned by this handle.
        unsafe {
            return ptr.read_volatile(self.address as *const T)
        }
    }

    pub fn write(&mut self, value: T) {
        // SAFETY: `at` guarantees a valid register owned by this handle.
        unsafe {
            ptr.write_volatile(self.address as *mut T, value)
        }
    }

    // Read-modify-write: clear the `clear` bits, then set the `set` bits.
    // Not atomic with respect to interrupts; wrap shared registers in a
    // `Critical` section or use the chip's set/clear aliases instead.
    pub fn modify(&mut self, clear: T, set: T) {
        value := self.read()
        self.write((value & ~clear) | set)
    }

    pub fn set_bits(&mut self, mask: T) {
        value := self.read()
        self.write(value | mask)
    }

    pub fn clear_bits(&mut self, mask: T) {
        value := self.read()
        self.write(value & ~mask)
    }

    // True when any bit in `mask` reads as one.
    pub fn any(&self, mask: T) -> bool {
        zero: T = 0
        return (self.read() & mask) != zero
    }

    // The value of the field `mask`.
    pub fn get(&self, mask: T) -> T {
        return decode(mask, self.read())
    }

    // Read-modify-write of the field `mask`. Traps if `value` does not fit.
    pub fn set(&mut self, mask: T, value: T) {
        bits := encode(mask, value)
        self.modify(mask, bits)
    }

    // Poll until every bit in `mask` reads one, at most `attempts` reads.
    // Use it for ready flags; a stuck device then yields Error.Timeout
    // instead of hanging forever.
    pub fn wait_set(&self, mask: T, attempts: u32) -> void!Error {
        for _ in 0..attempts {
            if (self.read() & mask) == mask {
                return ok()
            }
        }
        return err(Error.Timeout)
    }

    // Poll until every bit in `mask` reads zero, at most `attempts` reads.
    pub fn wait_clear(&self, mask: T, attempts: u32) -> void!Error {
        zero: T = 0
        for _ in 0..attempts {
            if (self.read() & mask) == zero {
                return ok()
            }
        }
        return err(Error.Timeout)
    }
}

// A peripheral's 32-bit registers at byte offsets from one base address.
// Implements Registers, so drivers in
// `std/embedded/peripheral` reach the hardware through it. Every access is
// one volatile 32-bit load or store, like `Reg<u32>`. Move-only: owning it is
// the permission to use the whole block.
pub struct RegisterBlock: Registers {
    base: usize

    // SAFETY: `base` must be the 4-aligned base address of one peripheral on
    // this target, every offset its driver uses must be a valid 32-bit
    // register of it, and no other handle or code may access those registers
    // in a conflicting way while this handle exists.
    pub unsafe fn at(base: usize) -> RegisterBlock {
        return RegisterBlock { base }
    }

    pub fn base(&self) -> usize {
        return self.base
    }

    pub fn read(&mut self, offset: usize) -> u32 {
        // SAFETY: `at` guarantees a valid register block owned by this handle.
        unsafe {
            return ptr.read_volatile((self.base + offset) as *const u32)
        }
    }

    pub fn write(&mut self, offset: usize, value: u32) {
        // SAFETY: `at` guarantees a valid register block owned by this handle.
        unsafe {
            ptr.write_volatile((self.base + offset) as *mut u32, value)
        }
    }
}

// Masks interrupts on the current core while it is alive and restores the
// previous state when dropped, so sections nest correctly:
//
//     section := hal.Critical.enter()
//     unsafe { TICKS += 1 }
//     core.drop(section)
//
// Bare-metal Cortex-M and RISC-V mask interrupts; hosted OS targets have no
// interrupts visible to programs and make this a no-op. It never excludes
// other threads or cores; use `std/sync` for those.
pub struct Critical {
    were_enabled: bool

    pub fn enter() -> Critical {
        return Critical { were_enabled: cpu.disable_interrupts() }
    }

    fn drop(&mut self) {
        // SAFETY: restores exactly the state saved by the matching enter.
        unsafe {
            cpu.restore_interrupts(self.were_enabled)
        }
    }
}

// A slot for an interrupt handler registered at run time. The vector-table
// entry point stays an `extern "C"` function with the interrupt's name and
// calls `run`, so firmware can switch handlers without rebuilding:
//
//     static mut TIMER: hal.Handler = hal.NO_HANDLER
//
//     extern "C" fn TIMER_IRQ_0() {
//         unsafe { TIMER.run() }
//     }
//
//     section := hal.Critical.enter()
//     unsafe { TIMER.set(on_tick) }
//     core.drop(section)
//
// A handler is a plain function pointer, so it captures no locals and stays
// valid for the whole program; it keeps state in `static mut` storage. Change
// a shared slot inside a critical section, which also orders the store before
// the next interrupt on this core.
pub struct Handler {
    function: Option<fn()>

    pub fn set(&mut self, function: fn()) {
        self.function = some(function)
    }
    pub fn clear(&mut self) {
        self.function = none
    }
    pub fn is_set(&self) -> bool {
        match &self.function {
            some(_) => true,
            none => false,
        }
    }
    // Calls the registered handler; false when the slot is empty.
    pub fn run(&self) -> bool {
        match &self.function {
            some(function) => {
                function()
                true
            },
            none => false,
        }
    }
}

// An empty handler slot, for initializing `static mut` storage.
pub const NO_HANDLER: Handler = Handler { function: none }

// Drive an output pin to `high`.
pub fn write_pin<P: OutputPin>(pin: &mut P, high: bool) {
    if high {
        pin.set_high()
    } else {
        pin.set_low()
    }
}

pub fn delay_ms<D: Delay>(delay: &mut D, milliseconds: u32) {
    for _ in 0..milliseconds {
        delay.delay_us(1000)
    }
}

// `time + microseconds`, or the largest time if that does not fit.
fn later(time: u64, microseconds: u64) -> u64 {
    if microseconds > 0xFFFF_FFFF_FFFF_FFFFu64 - time {
        return 0xFFFF_FFFF_FFFF_FFFFu64
    }
    return time + microseconds
}

// The microseconds a counter has run since `since_us`, one of its own
// now_us times; 0 for a time still ahead.
pub fn elapsed_us<C: Counter>(counter: &C, since_us: u64) -> u64 {
    now := counter.now_us()
    if now <= since_us {
        return 0
    }
    return now - since_us
}

// Busy-wait until a counter reaches `time_us`.
pub fn wait_until<C: Counter>(counter: &C, time_us: u64) {
    for counter.now_us() < time_us {}
}

// Fire an alarm `microseconds` from now. InvalidInput if that lies beyond
// the alarm's reach.
pub fn schedule_in_us<A: Alarm>(alarm: &mut A, microseconds: u64) -> void!Error {
    time := later(alarm.now_us(), microseconds)
    return alarm.schedule_at_us(time)
}

// Busy-wait until an alarm fires, then clear it.
pub fn wait_for_alarm<A: Alarm>(alarm: &mut A) {
    for !alarm.fired() {}
    alarm.clear()
}

// A time on a counter by which something must have happened, for drivers
// that poll a device and give up on one that is stuck:
//
//     deadline := hal.Deadline.after(timer, 10_000)
//     for !ready(bus)? {
//         if deadline.passed(timer) {
//             return err(hal.Error.Timeout)
//         }
//     }
//
// It holds only the time, so it never borrows the counter, and the same
// counter can delay between checks.
pub struct Deadline {
    time: u64

    // The deadline at `time_us`, a now_us time of the counter it is checked
    // against.
    pub fn at(time_us: u64) -> Deadline {
        return Deadline { time: time_us }
    }

    // The deadline `microseconds` from the counter's present time.
    pub fn after<C: Counter>(counter: &C, microseconds: u64) -> Deadline {
        return Deadline { time: later(counter.now_us(), microseconds) }
    }

    pub fn time_us(&self) -> u64 {
        return self.time
    }

    // True once the counter has reached the deadline.
    pub fn passed<C: Counter>(&self, counter: &C) -> bool {
        return counter.now_us() >= self.time
    }

    // The microseconds left until the deadline; 0 once it has passed.
    pub fn remaining_us<C: Counter>(&self, counter: &C) -> u64 {
        now := counter.now_us()
        if now >= self.time {
            return 0
        }
        return self.time - now
    }

    // Busy-wait until the counter reaches the deadline.
    pub fn wait<C: Counter>(&self, counter: &C) {
        wait_until(counter, self.time)
    }
}

// Ticks at a fixed period on a counter, for work that repeats, such as
// sampling a sensor every 10 ms. Each tick falls a whole number of periods
// after the start, so the ticks do not drift however late they are seen. A
// tick missed entirely, because the program was busy for longer than a
// period, is skipped rather than delivered late.
pub struct Ticker {
    next: u64
    period: u64

    // Ticks every `period_us`, at least 1, the first one period from now.
    pub fn start<C: Counter>(counter: &C, period_us: u64) -> Ticker {
        core.assert(period_us > 0)
        return Ticker { next: later(counter.now_us(), period_us), period: period_us }
    }

    pub fn period_us(&self) -> u64 {
        return self.period
    }

    // The counter time of the next tick.
    pub fn next_us(&self) -> u64 {
        return self.next
    }

    // True once per tick: when the counter has reached the next tick, which
    // then moves on to the first tick still ahead.
    pub fn ready<C: Counter>(&mut self, counter: &C) -> bool {
        now := counter.now_us()
        if now < self.next {
            return false
        }
        missed := (now - self.next) / self.period
        self.next = later(self.next, (missed + 1) * self.period)
        return true
    }

    // Busy-wait for the next tick.
    pub fn wait<C: Counter>(&mut self, counter: &C) {
        for !self.ready(counter) {}
    }
}

// Set a PWM output high for `numerator / denominator` of each period,
// rounded to the nearest step the output has. InvalidInput if the
// denominator is zero or below the numerator.
pub fn set_duty_fraction<P: PwmOutput>(output: &mut P, numerator: u32, denominator: u32) -> void!Error {
    if denominator == 0 || numerator > denominator {
        return err(Error.InvalidInput)
    }
    steps := output.max_duty() as u64
    duty := (numerator as u64 * steps + denominator as u64 / 2) / denominator as u64
    return output.set_duty(duty as u16)
}

// Set a PWM output high for `percent` of each period, 0 to 100.
pub fn set_duty_percent<P: PwmOutput>(output: &mut P, percent: u32) -> void!Error {
    return set_duty_fraction(output, percent, 100)
}

// Make a PWM output's pulses `pulse_us` long, for an output whose period is
// `period_us`: a hobby servo, for example, expects a 50 Hz output (a period
// of 20_000 us) and turns from one end to the other as the pulse goes from
// about 1000 to 2000 us. InvalidInput if the pulse is longer than the
// period.
pub fn set_pulse_us<P: PwmOutput>(output: &mut P, pulse_us: u32, period_us: u32) -> void!Error {
    return set_duty_fraction(output, pulse_us, period_us)
}

// One read of an analog input as a voltage in millivolts, for a converter
// whose reference is `reference_mv`, such as 3300 for a 3.3 V supply.
pub fn read_millivolts<A: AnalogInput>(input: &mut A, reference_mv: u32) -> u32!Error {
    value := input.read()? as u64
    full := input.max_value() as u64
    if full == 0 {
        return err(Error.Unsupported)
    }
    return ok(((value * reference_mv as u64 + full / 2) / full) as u32)
}

// Most I2C sensors expose numbered 8-bit registers. These helpers implement
// the common "write register number, then read or write data" transactions.

pub fn read_register<B: I2c>(bus: &mut B, address: u8, register: u8) -> u8!Error {
    command: [1]u8 = [register]
    value: [1]u8 = [0]
    bus.write_read(address, &command, &mut value)?
    return ok(value[0])
}

pub fn read_registers<B: I2c>(bus: &mut B, address: u8, first: u8, buffer: &mut[u8]) -> void!Error {
    command: [1]u8 = [first]
    return bus.write_read(address, &command, buffer)
}

pub fn write_register<B: I2c>(bus: &mut B, address: u8, register: u8, value: u8) -> void!Error {
    command: [2]u8 = [register, value]
    return bus.write(address, &command)
}

// One device on a shared SPI bus, selected by an active-low chip-select pin.
// The device owns its pin; the bus is passed to each transaction, so several
// devices share one bus and the borrow checker keeps transactions apart.
//
// The poll_* methods run the same transactions on a SpiPoll bus without
// waiting: the device stays selected from the first poll until the
// transaction finishes, fails, or is cancelled, and other devices must not
// use the bus meanwhile.
pub struct SpiDevice<P: OutputPin> {
    select: P
    // Of a polled transaction: 0 when none runs, 1 while the first or only
    // part runs, 2 while write_read's read runs.
    phase: u32

    // Takes the chip-select pin and deselects the device.
    pub fn new(select: P) -> SpiDevice<P> {
        device := SpiDevice<P> { select, phase: 0 }
        device.select.set_high()
        return device
    }

    // Advance a full-duplex exchange in place with the device selected;
    // see SpiPoll.poll_transfer.
    pub fn poll_transfer<B: SpiPoll>(&mut self, bus: &mut B, bytes: &mut[u8]) -> bool!Error {
        self.begin()
        result := bus.poll_transfer(bytes)
        return self.end(result)
    }

    // Advance a write with the device selected; see SpiPoll.poll_write.
    pub fn poll_write<B: SpiPoll>(&mut self, bus: &mut B, bytes: &[u8]) -> bool!Error {
        self.begin()
        result := bus.poll_write(bytes)
        return self.end(result)
    }

    // Advance a write of `command` and then a read of `buffer.len` bytes in
    // the same selection, as write_read does.
    pub fn poll_write_read<B: SpiPoll>(&mut self, bus: &mut B, command: &[u8], buffer: &mut[u8]) -> bool!Error {
        self.begin()
        if self.phase == 1 {
            match bus.poll_write(command) {
                ok(done) => {
                    if !done {
                        return ok(false)
                    }
                }
                err(error) => {
                    self.deselect()
                    return err(error)
                }
            }
            self.phase = 2
            for index in 0..buffer.len {
                buffer[index] = 0
            }
        }
        result := bus.poll_transfer(buffer)
        return self.end(result)
    }

    // Abandon a polled transaction, if one runs, and deselect the device.
    pub fn cancel<B: SpiPoll>(&mut self, bus: &mut B) {
        if self.phase != 0 {
            bus.cancel()
            self.deselect()
        }
    }

    // True while a polled transaction runs.
    pub fn is_busy(&self) -> bool {
        return self.phase != 0
    }

    fn begin(&mut self) {
        if self.phase == 0 {
            self.select.set_low()
            self.phase = 1
        }
    }

    fn end(&mut self, result: bool!Error) -> bool!Error {
        match result {
            ok(done) => {
                if done {
                    self.deselect()
                }
                return ok(done)
            }
            err(error) => {
                self.deselect()
                return err(error)
            }
        }
    }

    fn deselect(&mut self) {
        self.select.set_high()
        self.phase = 0
    }

    // Full-duplex exchange in place with the device selected.
    pub fn transfer<B: Spi>(&mut self, bus: &mut B, bytes: &mut[u8]) -> void!Error {
        self.select.set_low()
        result := bus.transfer(bytes)
        self.select.set_high()
        return result
    }

    pub fn write<B: Spi>(&mut self, bus: &mut B, bytes: &[u8]) -> void!Error {
        self.select.set_low()
        result := bus.write(bytes)
        self.select.set_high()
        return result
    }

    // Write `command`, then read `buffer.len` bytes in the same selection.
    pub fn write_read<B: Spi>(&mut self, bus: &mut B, command: &[u8], buffer: &mut[u8]) -> void!Error {
        self.select.set_low()
        match bus.write(command) {
            ok() => {}
            err(error) => {
                self.select.set_high()
                return err(error)
            }
        }
        for index in 0..buffer.len {
            buffer[index] = 0
        }
        result := bus.transfer(buffer)
        self.select.set_high()
        return result
    }

    // Give back the chip-select pin.
    pub fn release(self) -> P {
        let SpiDevice { select, phase } = self
        return select
    }
}

// --- Blocking from polling -------------------------------------------------

// Polls a blocking adapter or helper makes before it gives up with Timeout:
// far more than any transaction a device answers takes (a 256-byte I2C
// transfer at 100 kHz is about 25 ms, tens of thousands of polls), so only
// a stuck bus reaches it. NO_POLL_LIMIT waits as long as it takes.
pub const DEFAULT_POLL_LIMIT: u32 = 50_000_000
pub const NO_POLL_LIMIT: u32 = 0

fn out_of_polls(polls: &mut u32, limit: u32) -> bool {
    *polls += 1
    return limit != NO_POLL_LIMIT && *polls >= limit
}

// An I2C bus with the blocking I2c protocol over any I2cPoll
// implementation: each transaction polls until it finishes, and after
// DEFAULT_POLL_LIMIT polls (or set_poll_limit's) cancels it and reports
// Timeout. A controller that implements only the polling form thereby
// serves every blocking driver too.
pub struct BlockingI2c<B: I2cPoll>: I2c {
    bus: B
    limit: u32

    pub fn new(bus: B) -> BlockingI2c<B> {
        return BlockingI2c<B> { bus, limit: DEFAULT_POLL_LIMIT }
    }

    // Give up after `polls` polls per transaction; NO_POLL_LIMIT never
    // does.
    pub fn set_poll_limit(&mut self, polls: u32) {
        self.limit = polls
    }

    pub fn write(&mut self, address: u8, bytes: &[u8]) -> void!Error {
        polls := 0u32
        for !self.bus.poll_write(address, bytes)? {
            if out_of_polls(&mut polls, self.limit) {
                self.bus.cancel()
                return err(Error.Timeout)
            }
        }
        return ok()
    }

    pub fn read(&mut self, address: u8, buffer: &mut[u8]) -> void!Error {
        polls := 0u32
        for !self.bus.poll_read(address, buffer)? {
            if out_of_polls(&mut polls, self.limit) {
                self.bus.cancel()
                return err(Error.Timeout)
            }
        }
        return ok()
    }

    pub fn write_read(&mut self, address: u8, bytes: &[u8], buffer: &mut[u8]) -> void!Error {
        polls := 0u32
        for !self.bus.poll_write_read(address, bytes, buffer)? {
            if out_of_polls(&mut polls, self.limit) {
                self.bus.cancel()
                return err(Error.Timeout)
            }
        }
        return ok()
    }

    // The bus, for its own methods.
    pub fn bus(&mut self) -> &mut B {
        return &mut self.bus
    }

    pub fn release(self) -> B {
        let BlockingI2c { bus, limit } = self
        return bus
    }
}

// An SPI bus with the blocking Spi protocol over any SpiPoll
// implementation, as BlockingI2c is for I2C.
pub struct BlockingSpi<B: SpiPoll>: Spi {
    bus: B
    limit: u32

    pub fn new(bus: B) -> BlockingSpi<B> {
        return BlockingSpi<B> { bus, limit: DEFAULT_POLL_LIMIT }
    }

    pub fn set_poll_limit(&mut self, polls: u32) {
        self.limit = polls
    }

    pub fn transfer(&mut self, bytes: &mut[u8]) -> void!Error {
        polls := 0u32
        for !self.bus.poll_transfer(bytes)? {
            if out_of_polls(&mut polls, self.limit) {
                self.bus.cancel()
                return err(Error.Timeout)
            }
        }
        return ok()
    }

    pub fn write(&mut self, bytes: &[u8]) -> void!Error {
        polls := 0u32
        for !self.bus.poll_write(bytes)? {
            if out_of_polls(&mut polls, self.limit) {
                self.bus.cancel()
                return err(Error.Timeout)
            }
        }
        return ok()
    }

    pub fn bus(&mut self) -> &mut B {
        return &mut self.bus
    }

    pub fn release(self) -> B {
        let BlockingSpi { bus, limit } = self
        return bus
    }
}

// Wait for a DMA buffer transfer to finish; errors as for poll_finish.
pub fn finish_transfer<T: DmaBuffer>(transfer: &mut T) -> void!Error {
    for !transfer.poll_finish()? {}
    return ok()
}

// Wait for the next measurement of a PWM input, polling at most `polls`
// times (NO_POLL_LIMIT for no limit); Timeout after that.
pub fn capture<P: PwmInput>(input: &mut P, polls: u32) -> PulseCapture!Error {
    capture := PulseCapture.new()
    count := 0u32
    for !input.poll_capture(&mut capture)? {
        if out_of_polls(&mut count, polls) {
            return err(Error.Timeout)
        }
    }
    return ok(capture)
}

// Send a CAN frame, waiting for a free transmit buffer for at most `polls`
// polls (NO_POLL_LIMIT for no limit); Timeout after that, as on a bus where
// no other node acknowledges frames, so none ever leaves.
pub fn send_frame<C: Can>(can: &mut C, frame: &CanFrame, polls: u32) -> void!Error {
    count := 0u32
    for !can.poll_send(frame)? {
        if out_of_polls(&mut count, polls) {
            return err(Error.Timeout)
        }
    }
    return ok()
}

// Wait for a CAN frame, for at most `polls` polls; Timeout after that.
pub fn receive_frame<C: Can>(can: &mut C, frame: &mut CanFrame, polls: u32) -> void!Error {
    count := 0u32
    for !can.poll_receive(frame)? {
        if out_of_polls(&mut count, polls) {
            return err(Error.Timeout)
        }
    }
    return ok()
}

// Queue all of `samples` on an I2S output, waiting for room; Timeout after
// `polls` polls without progress (NO_POLL_LIMIT for no limit).
pub fn write_samples<O: I2sOutput>(output: &mut O, samples: &[i32], polls: u32) -> void!Error {
    sent := 0usize
    count := 0u32
    for sent < samples.len {
        taken := output.poll_write(samples[sent..])?
        if taken == 0 {
            if out_of_polls(&mut count, polls) {
                return err(Error.Timeout)
            }
        } else {
            sent += taken
            count = 0
        }
    }
    return ok()
}

// Fill all of `samples` from an I2S input, waiting for them; Timeout after
// `polls` polls without progress.
pub fn read_samples<I: I2sInput>(input: &mut I, samples: &mut[i32], polls: u32) -> void!Error {
    received := 0usize
    count := 0u32
    for received < samples.len {
        taken := input.poll_read(&mut samples[received..])?
        if taken == 0 {
            if out_of_polls(&mut count, polls) {
                return err(Error.Timeout)
            }
        } else {
            received += taken
            count = 0
        }
    }
    return ok()
}

// A 16-bit sample as an I2S sample, and an I2S sample's top 16 bits.
pub fn sample_from_i16(value: i16) -> i32 {
    return (value as i32) << 16
}

pub fn sample_to_i16(sample: i32) -> i16 {
    return (sample >> 16) as i16
}
